Link targets
description: the parens hold a yamlover expression - sigiled pointers, the bare alias, external URLs
- >
  The parenthesized target is a **yamlover expression**. Canonically it is a *pointer* — the same
  `*`-sigiled grammar as [pointers](*::language:pointers) everywhere else, addressing the app's
  **instance space** (the space the tree, the breadcrumbs, and the URL navigate):
- !!<*yamlover: $defs: table>
  header:
    - Target
    - 'Anchored at'
      width: 2
  - ['`*:: a: b`', 'the **project root** (the directory yamlover was served)']
  - ['`*: a: b`, `*: 2`', 'the **document** the link appears in (the nearest yamlover entity)']
  - ['`*..: sib`', 'the **parent** of the mapping the prose belongs to']
  - ['`*name`', 'the mapping the prose belongs to (**current** scope)']
  - ['`&...`', '**reserved** - a bookmark target; parsed, not yet resolved']
  - ['`scheme://...`, `mailto:...`', 'an ordinary **external** link, opened in a new tab']
- >
  Inside prose the pointer is written **compact** (`*::a:b`) - a `: `-spaced spelling inside a
  bare scalar line would re-split the line as a key and value, and compact is valid in every
  context. A position is the **bare integer** segment (`*:2`, `*::a:0`); the retired bracket
  index reads forever as an alias, written never.
- >
  **Presentational containers are transparent frames.** A `bullets`/`numbered` item and a table
  cell exist to render, not to scope: `*name` and `*..: x` there mean exactly what they mean in
  a plain `- >` block - the wrapper hands its own frame through. A *structural* cell (an
  untagged container cell folds as a chapter) is a real frame and resets normally. A link that
  still resolves to nothing is a **doctor warning** (`link/dead-target`), logged at startup and
  on every reconcile, and renders visibly broken - never as silent plain text.
- >
  The **bare colon form** - `(::a:b)`, `(:a:b)` - is the same pointer without its sigil, and
  reads **forever** as an alias; the sigiled form is what the engine emits when a
  [move](*::server) rewrites a link. The legacy slash spellings (`/a/b`
  document-relative, `//a/b` project-rooted) still navigate but are frozen - written never.
  Anything else does not resolve, and the label renders as plain text.
  `examples/69-marklower-links.yo` is a tour of the flavours.
- >
  A `&...` target is the **bookmark** half of the expression space, reserved for the annotations
  rework: it parses (a move that strands one *reports* it rather than silently breaking it), but
  the client does not yet navigate it. *TODO: give bookmark links behavior when annotations are
  refactored.*
- >
  One seam decides what a target means - `parseLinkTarget`, shared verbatim by the client's
  `resolveLink` (navigation) and the engine's move planner (rewriting) - so a spelling that
  navigates is exactly a spelling a move keeps alive.
